#### Step 1: Configure Shell Application (Host)

The shell application acts as the Module Federation host, loading remote modules:

**Webpack Configuration:**

```javascript
// shell/webpack.config.js
const ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');

module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index',
  mode: 'production',
  output: {
    publicPath: 'https://cdn.gainwell.com/shell/',
    uniqueName: 'shell',
  },
  plugins: [
    new ModuleFederationPlugin({
      name: 'shell',
      remotes: {
        claimsMfe: 'claimsMfe@https://cdn.gainwell.com/mfe/claims/latest/remoteEntry.js',
        providerMfe: 'providerMfe@https://cdn.gainwell.com/mfe/provider/latest/remoteEntry.js',
        eligibilityMfe: 'eligibilityMfe@https://cdn.gainwell.com/mfe/eligibility/latest/remoteEntry.js',
      },
      shared: {
        react: {
          singleton: true,              // Only one React instance allowed
          requiredVersion: '^18.2.0',    // Compatible version range
          eager: true,                   // Load immediately with shell (don't lazy load)
        },
        'react-dom': {
          singleton: true,
          requiredVersion: '^18.2.0',
          eager: true,
        },
        'react-router-dom': {
          singleton: true,
          requiredVersion: '^6.8.0',
        },
        '@gainwell/design-system': {
          singleton: true,
          requiredVersion: '^2.0.0',
        },
      },
    }),
  ],
};
```

**Key Configuration Options:**

- **`name`**: Unique identifier for this host application
- **`remotes`**: Map of remote module names to their remote entry URLs
- **`shared`**: Dependencies to share across host and remotes
  - **`singleton: true`**: Only one instance allowed (critical for React)
  - **`requiredVersion`**: Semantic version range remotes must satisfy
  - **`eager: true`**: Load with host immediately (for critical dependencies like React)

**Shell Application Code:**

```typescript
// shell/src/App.tsx
import React, { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';

// Lazy load remote modules
const ClaimsMFE = lazy(() => import('claimsMfe/App'));
const ProviderMFE = lazy(() => import('providerMfe/App'));
const EligibilityMFE = lazy(() => import('eligibilityMfe/App'));

export function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <div>
        <Header />
        <Suspense fallback={<LoadingSpinner />}>
          <Routes>
            <Route path="/claims/*" element={<ClaimsMFE context={mfeContext} />} />
            <Route path="/provider/*" element={<ProviderMFE context={mfeContext} />} />
            <Route path="/eligibility/*" element={<EligibilityMFE context={mfeContext} />} />
          </Routes>
        </Suspense>
        <Footer />
      </div>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}
```

**Dynamic Remote Configuration (Advanced):**

For production environments, remote URLs should be configurable at runtime:

```typescript
// shell/src/bootstrap.tsx
import { loadRemoteModule } from './moduleFederation';

async function initializeApp() {
  // Fetch remote module configuration from backend
  const config = await fetch('/api/mfe-config').then(r => r.json());

  // Dynamically configure remotes
  const claimsMfe = await loadRemoteModule({
    url: config.remotes.claims.url,
    scope: 'claimsMfe',
    module: './App',
  });

  // Render app with dynamically loaded modules
  ReactDOM.render(<App claimsMfe={claimsMfe} />, document.getElementById('root'));
}

initializeApp();
```

